<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- A round-trip case: this source is built to PDF, the PDF is transcribed, and the     -->
<!-- transcription is compared with this source.  Written for the purpose; nothing here -->
<!-- is taken from any publication.  Exercises: theorem-like blocks on one counter      -->
<!-- within sections, a remark, attached and detached proofs, numbered and unnumbered   -->
<!-- displays, aligned rows, cases, cross-references of every kind, a footnote, lists.  -->
<pretext xml:lang="en-US">
    <docinfo>
        <macros>
        </macros>
    </docinfo>

    <article xml:id="article-parity">
        <title>Parity of sums and products</title>

        <frontmatter>
            <bibinfo>
                <author>
                    <personname>Example Author</personname>
                    <institution>Department of Examples, University of Somewhere</institution>
                    <email>author@example.org</email>
                </author>
                <date>March 1, 2026</date>
            </bibinfo>
            <titlepage>
                <titlepage-items/>
            </titlepage>
            <abstract>
                <p>
                    We record elementary facts about the parity of sums and products of integers.
                    Nothing here is new; the article exists to exercise a transcription.
                </p>
            </abstract>
        </frontmatter>

        <section xml:id="section-introduction">
            <title>Introduction</title>

            <p>
                The parity of an integer is the first invariant a student meets <xref ref="biblio-author-first-course"/>.
                Throughout, <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> denotes the integers<fn>We count zero as an even integer.</fn> and <m>n</m>, <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> are integers.
                In <xref ref="section-even-odd"/> we fix the definitions and prove <xref ref="theorem-parity-sum"/>, which a referee once called <q>the least surprising theorem in the subject.</q>
                Products are treated in <xref ref="section-products"/>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Two conventions are used without comment:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            every variable is an integer unless we say otherwise;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <q>iff</q> abbreviates <q>if and only if.</q>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </section>

        <section xml:id="section-even-odd">
            <title>Even and odd integers</title>

            <definition xml:id="definition-even-odd">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        An integer <m>n</m> is <term>even</term> if <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>, and <term>odd</term> if <m>n = 2k + 1</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

            <lemma xml:id="lemma-even-sum">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The sum of two even integers is even.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <proof>
                    <p>
                        If <m>a = 2j</m> and <m>b = 2k</m> then <m>a + b = 2(j + k)</m>.
                    </p>
                </proof>
            </lemma>

            <theorem xml:id="theorem-parity-sum">
                <title>Parity of a sum</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, the sum <m>a + b</m> is even iff <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> have the same parity.
                        Equivalently,
                        <md xml:id="equation-sign-multiplicative" number="yes">
                            (-1)^{a+b} = (-1)^a (-1)^b.
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </theorem>

            <remark xml:id="remark-sign">
                <p>
                    The identity <xref ref="equation-sign-multiplicative"/> says that <m>n \mapsto (-1)^n</m> is a homomorphism from <m>(\mathbb{Z}, +)</m> to <m>(\{1, -1\}, \cdot)</m>.
                </p>
            </remark>

            <p>
                The lemma handles one of the four cases.
                We give the whole argument, since it is short.
            </p>

            <proof ref="theorem-parity-sum">
                <p>
                    Write <m>a = 2j + r</m> and <m>b = 2k + s</m> with <m>r, s \in \{0, 1\}</m>.
                    Then
                    <md>
                        a + b = 2(j + k) + (r + s),
                    </md>
                    and <m>r + s</m> is even exactly when <m>r = s</m>.
                    The case <m>r = s = 0</m> is <xref ref="lemma-even-sum"/>.
                </p>
            </proof>

            <corollary xml:id="corollary-odd-count">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        A sum of integers is odd iff an odd number of its terms are odd.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>

            <example xml:id="example-three-terms">
                <p>
                    In <m>3 + 4 + 7 = 14</m> two terms are odd, and the sum is even, as <xref ref="corollary-odd-count"/> predicts.
                </p>
            </example>
        </section>

        <section xml:id="section-products">
            <title>Products</title>

            <proposition xml:id="proposition-product">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers.
                        <ol marker="(a)">
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    If either is even, then <m>ab</m> is even.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    If both are odd, then <m>ab</m> is odd.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <proof>
                    <p>
                        For the second part,
                        <md>
                            <mrow xml:id="equation-odd-product-expanded" number="yes">(2j + 1)(2k + 1) \amp = 4jk + 2j + 2k + 1</mrow>
                            <mrow xml:id="equation-odd-product-factored" number="yes">\amp = 2(2jk + j + k) + 1.</mrow>
                        </md>
                        The first part is immediate from <xref ref="definition-even-odd"/>.
                    </p>
                </proof>
            </proposition>

            <p>
                Combining <xref ref="equation-odd-product-factored"/> with <xref ref="theorem-parity-sum"/>, the parity <m>\pi(n) \in \{0, 1\}</m> of <m>n</m>, defined by
                <md>
                    \pi(n) = \begin{cases} 0 \amp n \text{ even} \\ 1 \amp n \text{ odd} \end{cases},
                </md>
                satisfies <m>\pi(a + b) \equiv \pi(a) + \pi(b)</m> and <m>\pi(ab) = \pi(a)\pi(b)</m>; see also <xref ref="biblio-writer-reader-parity"/>.
            </p>

            <paragraphs xml:id="paragraphs-acknowledgements">
                <title>Acknowledgements</title>

                <p>
                    The author thanks nobody in particular.
                </p>
            </paragraphs>
        </section>

